# Anonymised wall-remote capture — derived from a real one, not itself one.
#
# REAL, verbatim from a capture taken 2026-08-15 from a physical Somfy wall remote,
# received by an ESP32-S3 + CC1101 running ESPSomfy-RTS v2.5.6:
#   - the first 7 durations below: the wake-up HIGH, the silence after it,
#     2 hardware-sync halves and the software-sync HIGH, exactly as measured;
#   - the key byte 0xAD and the command (Up), neither of which names a remote;
#   - every half-symbol deviation from the nominal 640 µs used in the body: each is
#     one of the 52 deviations this same capture produced on its own single
#     half-symbol segments (-46..57 µs), applied in measured order and
#     cycled to cover the frame.
#
# SYNTHETIC, because the original's pulse train encoded a real remote's address and
# its rolling code at the moment of capture:
#   - address 0x00C0DE — this project's bring-up address, not a remote's;
#   - rolling code 1;
#   - and so, downstream of those, the checksum, the obfuscation chain, the 56 bits
#     themselves, and the merged-segment structure that follows from them.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE THEREFORE CANNOT SHOW: that our checksum and de-obfuscation agree
# with Somfy's. Those bits are ours now. It still pins them against *change*, since
# the bytes are frozen here, but that is regression cover, not interoperability.
#
# Rewritten 2026-08-19 by `cargo run -p xtask -- anonymise-capture`. The method, and
# what it does and does not preserve, is in fixtures/README.md. Do not hand-edit:
# the durations are evidence and the original is gone.
#
# command=Up bits=56 hwsync=4 pulseCount=92 (the original was
# 89; an edge count follows from the payload bits, so it moved with them)
# body total 72027 µs against the original's 71996 µs
# durations-only, one per line; see fixtures/README.md
